            <ArticleTitle>Tonic-clonic seizures in a postpartum patient: case report.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>307-5</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.4037/ajcc2010668</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Eclamptic seizures are a known complication of pregnancy; less well publicized is the patient with late postpartum eclampsia. Late postpartum eclamptic seizures are convulsions that occur more then 48 hours after delivery. Because 40% of patients who have late postpartum eclampsia do not experience preeclampsia, clinical awareness is essential for early treatment and care.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
